                      "id": "1781267026624_Constraints",
                      "name": "Constraints",
                      "value": "Fill template placeholders during initial generation and keep styles email-safe.\nRules:\n- Do not leave visible template placeholders such as __headline_text__, __subheading_text__, __cta_text__, __section_heading_text__, __section_intro_text__, __body_bullet_1__, or angle-bracket placeholder labels in the generated asset.\n- Treat placeholder names as semantic hints. Replace heading, subheading, CTA, body, bullet, image-alt, and support-text placeholders with customer-ready copy based on the user message, seeded Content, Audience, and sandbox configuration.\n- CTA text must be a real 2-5 word action-oriented label with sufficient contrast and padding.\n- CTA href/link placeholders must be replaced with the most relevant real URL from seeded content or the user message. If no real URL is available, use # for the href and remove tracking-parameter placeholders instead of displaying them.\n- Image URL placeholders should be replaced by a real supplied image URL when available; otherwise preserve the existing real template image URL or use the configured content image URL when supplied.\n- Preserve intentional runtime personalization tokens only when they use valid merge-token syntax such as {{FirstName}}. Do not preserve template scaffolding placeholders as visible copy.\n- Headline should be visually H1-like but implemented with email-safe <td> or <div> styling.\n- Subheading should be one short sentence that supports the headline.\n- Body copy should support 1-3 short paragraphs or bullets with comfortable line-height and padding.\n- Keep the main message above the CTA.\n- Use only one primary CTA.\n- Quote styling must remain readable in dark mode.\n- Avoid JavaScript, unsupported CSS, or image-only CTAs."
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "1781267026624_Template",
                      "name": "Template",
                      "value": "{{$context.$nodes.REFINE_EMAIL_TEMPLATES.$output.result || $context.$nodes.REFINE_LANDINGPAGE_TEMPLATES.$output.result}}"
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "1781267026624_MandatoryConstraints",
                      "name": "MandatoryConstraints",
                      "value": "Use the following constraints as mandatory rules when converting or generating email HTML.\nPreserve the existing email structure and compatibility. Do not alter the core table structure, layout containers, widths, or critical inline CSS unless explicitly instructed.\nOn first generation, replace template placeholder tokens with generated customer-ready content. Do not leave visible placeholder tokens such as __...__, <..._text>, or placeholder button labels in the rendered asset.\nFor personalization, preserve only valid runtime merge tokens such as {{FirstName}} when they are intentionally needed. Do not fabricate user data.\nRequired constraints:\n- Preserve table-based email layout.\n- Use inline CSS only.\n- Do not rely on external CSS, JavaScript, modern JS, or unsupported HTML features.\n- Target Outlook desktop, Gmail, Apple Mail, and mobile email clients.\n- Use web-safe fonts unless brand-approved fallbacks are provided.\n- Use UTF-8 encoding.\n- Keep total HTML weight lightweight; aim for under 100-150KB where feasible.\n- Avoid CSS background images unless supported with safe fallbacks.\n- Use bulletproof button patterns for CTAs.\n- Ensure CTAs are real <a href=\"...\"> links, not image-only buttons.\n- Include required legal disclaimers, terms, unsubscribe links, and preference-management links exactly as specified.\n- Preserve accessibility attributes, including meaningful alt text for images.\n- Do not embed critical text only inside images.\n- Do not invent tracking URLs, user attributes, offer details, legal copy, or personalization values.\n- Remove unresolved tracking parameter placeholders from URLs instead of showing placeholder text.\nReturn final HTML only unless asked to explain changes."
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "1781267026624_Audience",
                      "name": "Audience",
                      "value": "Buyer Role: Decision Maker, Persona: CIO, CHRO"
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "1781267026624_Content",
                      "name": "Content",
                      "value": "Oracle Cloud EPM helps Kraft Heinz master big market shiftsTitleOracle Cloud EPM helps Kraft Heinz master big market shiftsSubtitleThe food and beverage giant uses Oracle Cloud EPM to guide faster decision-making to stay on top of a changing market.Main Quote“We started with Oracle Cloud EPM Planning in North America with zero-based budgeting and expanded the planning footprint to link our global operations. We wanted to better incorporate financial reports and forecasts, do strategic planning, and implement proper processes. It gave us a better vision as to how we can increase revenue, reduce costs within the plants, look more deeply into our industrial costs, and see where we could make innovations.”Frank TagoeSenior Manager of IT Finance, The Kraft Heinz CompanyStorySince the 2015 merger of the Kraft and Heinz businesses, Kraft Heinz has gone on to become one of the world’s largest food and beverage companies. The $26 billion-a-year behemoth includes famed household products such as Kraft Macaroni & Cheese, Heinz Tomato Ketchup, Oscar Mayer meats, Maxwell House coffee, and Grey Poupon mustard.To better understand business performance, the Kraft Heinz finance team needed better and faster insights. It struggled with 10 different, decentralized tools that performed more or less the same tasks. The insights into the effectiveness of the company’s trade promotions and marketing spend would provide visibility into what products they should be selling and which products customers are drawn to. Kraft Heinz looked to Oracle Fusion Cloud EPM to gain this strong understanding of how the market was changing and reacting.Competitive AdvantageOracle Cloud EPM gave the Kraft Heinz finance team swift access to much-needed insights on sales forecasts, actual results, plus the costs of logistics and manufacturing. This crucial information allows the company to understand product-level profitability and cost management in ways it never knew before.ResultsMoving to Oracle Cloud EPM helped the finance team attain a single source of truth. In addition, the team was also able to achieve standardized processes. “It gave us a better vision as to how we can increase revenue, reduce costs within the plants, look more deeply into our industrial costs, and see where we could make innovations,” says Frank Tagoe, Kraft Heinz senior manager of IT finance.ChannelsEmail, Landing page, WebTagsOracle Cloud CPMCustomer NameThe Kraft Heinz CompanyIndustryFood ProductsFeatured Buyer RoleInfluencerLinkhttps://www.oracle.com/customers/kraft-heinz/"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": "1783000000002",
                  "code": "PREPARE_ASSET_GENERATION_DATA",
                  "type": "CODE",
                  "metadata": {
                    "name": "Prepare Asset Generation Data",
                    "returnType": "object",
                    "sourceCode": "const currentTacticItem = ($context.$nodes?.AUTOMATION_JSON_MODEL_BUILDER?.$currentItem) || {};\nconst automationModel = ($context.$variables && $context.$variables.automationContentJsonModel) || {};\nconst tacticObjective = automationModel.tacticObjective || currentTacticItem.objective || currentTacticItem.description || currentTacticItem.name || \"Create a customer-ready marketing asset.\";\n\nfunction asText(value) {\n  return value === null || value === undefined ? \"\" : String(value).trim();\n}\n\nfunction inferAssetType(item) {\n  const text = [item.type, item.name, item.channel, item.assetType].map(asText).join(\" \").toLowerCase();\n  if (/landing/.test(text)) return \"Landing Page\";\n  return \"Email\";\n}\n\nfunction inferProgramType(value) {\n  const text = asText(value).toLowerCase();\n  if (/renewal/.test(text)) return \"Renewal\";\n  if (/cross[-\\s]?sell/.test(text)) return \"Cross-sell\";\n  return \"Cross-sell\";\n}\n\nfunction inferProduct(value) {\n  const text = asText(value).toLowerCase();\n  if (/\\berp\\b|enterprise resource planning/.test(text)) return \"Oracle Fusion Cloud ERP\";\n  if (/\\bhcm\\b|human capital/.test(text)) return \"Oracle Fusion Cloud HCM\";\n  if (/\\bscm\\b|supply chain/.test(text)) return \"Oracle Fusion Cloud SCM\";\n  if (/epm|enterprise performance management/.test(text)) return \"Oracle Cloud EPM\";\n  if (/fusion marketing|marketing/.test(text)) return \"Fusion Marketing\";\n  return \"Fusion Marketing\";\n}\n\nconst sourceText = [\n  tacticObjective,\n  currentTacticItem.name,\n  currentTacticItem.description,\n  currentTacticItem.type,\n  currentTacticItem.channel,\n  $context.$system && $context.$system.$inputMessage\n].map(asText).filter(Boolean).join(\" \");\n\nconst result = {\n  inputMessage: asText($context.$system && $context.$system.$inputMessage),\n  tacticObjective: asText(tacticObjective),\n  currentTacticItem,\n  sandboxConfig: {\n    programType: inferProgramType(sourceText),\n    product: inferProduct(sourceText),\n    assetType: inferAssetType(currentTacticItem),\n    objective: asText(tacticObjective),\n    audience: \"Business decision makers\",\n    tone: \"Professional, concise, and brand-aligned\",\n    cta: /demo|request/.test(sourceText.toLowerCase()) ? \"Request a demo\" : \"Learn more\"\n  }\n};\n\nreturn result;"
                  },
                  "outcomes": {
                    "success": "1783000000003"
                  },
                  "inputs": [],
                  "diagram": {
                    "x": 5240.5,
                    "y": 9665
                  },
                  "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"required\": [\n    \"result\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}"
                },
                {
                  "id": "1783000000003",
                  "code": "GENERATE_ASSET_RESPONSE",
                  "type": "LLM",
                  "metadata": {
                    "name": "Generate Asset Response",
                    "chatHistoryEnabled": false
                  },
                  "outcomes": {
                    "success": "1781515639783"
                  },
                  "inputs": [
                    {
                      "id": "n330_prompt",
                      "name": "prompt",
                      "type": "string",
                      "value": "DATA:\n{{$context.$nodes.PREPARE_ASSET_GENERATION_DATA.$output.result}}\n\nUSER MESSAGE:\n{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n\nTACTIC OBJECTIVE:\n{{$context.$variables.automationContentJsonModel.tacticObjective}}\n\nCURRENT TACTIC ITEM:\n{{$context.$nodes.AUTOMATION_JSON_MODEL_BUILDER.$currentItem}}\n\nSEEDED CONTENT:\n{{$context.$variables.Content}}\n\nSEEDED TEMPLATE:\n{{$context.$variables.Template}}\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n{{$context.$variables.MandatoryConstraints}}\n{{$context.$variables.Constraints}}\n\nTASK:\nGenerate a customer-ready marketing asset message in the same structured format used by the previous message content node, wrapped in the workflow output object.\n\nReturn a JSON object with exactly these top-level fields:\n- result: an object with exactly title and content\n- timeout: false\n- error: \"\"\n\nIf CURRENT TACTIC ITEM name or type is \"landingPage\":\n1. result.title must be an empty string.\n2. result.content must be complete simple HTML for the landing page as a string in this format: \"<html><body>Html Content</body></html>\".\n\nIf CURRENT TACTIC ITEM name or type is \"email\" or \"followUpEmail\":\n1. result.title must be a concise email subject line based on the tactic objective, no more than 15 words.\n2. result.content must be the email body as complete simple HTML as a string in this format: \"<html><body>Message Content</body></html>\".\n\nUse DATA.sandboxConfig, seeded content, seeded template, constraints, user message, and tactic objective to make the copy specific and customer-ready. Replace visible template placeholders with real generated content. Preserve only intentional runtime personalization tokens such as {{FirstName}} when useful.\n\nValidation:\n- result.content must be plain HTML in a string.\n- result.content must not contain JavaScript, functions, unresolved placeholder labels, or markdown fences.\n- timeout must always be false.\n- error must always be an empty string.\n- Return only the final JSON object and nothing else.\n\nRequired JSON shape:\n{\n  \"result\": {\n    \"title\": \"Protect Your Investment: Maintenance Packages for Expiring Warranties\",\n    \"content\": \"<html><body>Dear Purchasing Manager,<br><br>Your account's warranty is about to expire. We invite you to visit our webpage to explore exclusive maintenance packages designed to keep your solar systems running efficiently.<br><br><a href=\"http://www.supremosolar.com/maintenance\">View Maintenance Packages</a><br><br>Best regards,<br>Supremo Solar Marketer</body></html>\"\n  },\n  \"timeout\": false,\n  \"error\": \"\"\n}"
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "n330_systemPrompt",
                      "name": "systemPrompt",
                      "type": "string",
                      "value": "Return only a valid JSON object with exactly three top-level fields: result, timeout, and error. result must contain exactly title and content. timeout must be false. error must be an empty string. Do not return markdown, an artifact preview widget, oraInfoDisplay, explanatory prose, or any additional fields."
                    }
                  ],
                  "diagram": {
